    "created_at": "2018-10-08T16:09:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-10-08T16:09:55Z",
    "body": "From what I can tell there are a few things going on here.\r\n\r\n- The current spec says that scrollable areas \"can\" be focusable areas. (It literally uses the word \"can\"; not sure how to interpret that.)\r\n- The current spec says that elements with their tabindex focus flag set \"can\" be focusable areas.\r\n- The spec differentiates between being a focusable area, and being reachable via sequential focus navigation (TAB key). These are independent.\r\n\r\nBut there's nothing that says that scrollable areas should have their tabindex focus flag set (i.e., what you refer to as tabIndex=0). And there's nothing saying that they should be reachable via sequential focus navigation. There's clauses that allow the UA to do either, if it wants, but nothing requiring it.\r\n\r\nIt could be nice to tighten up the spec in various ways. But I think the current intent is to allow UAs to make their own choices on different axes. So you can change Chrome, and maybe file FYI bugs on browsers, but not sure what we should do the spec level.",
